concept :the_squeeze do
  name "The Squeeze"
  aka "拓", "taking a face"
  tags :legibility, :water, :resistance, :biotech
  prominence :recognized

  prose <<~PROSE
    How a fired ceramic is read. #{ref :memory_ceramics, "Memory ceramics"} hold their pattern as relief through the body of the substrate — ridges, voids and hardness changes where fungal growth was arrested and mineralized — so a piece has no readable surface until one is cut. A reader exposes a single flat face, takes a damp-paper impression of it, and reads the paper.

    Nothing in the procedure needs power and nothing in it is alive, which is the property the #{ref :the_preservationists, "Preservationists"} chose the substrate for. A reading room can be walled, dried, salted and burned out, and the pieces come through it unchanged apart from what the plate took.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :mechanics, heading: "Face, Paper, Pounce"
    The face is lapped flat on a wetted iron plate, coarse grit and then fine, until the relief stands clean under a raking lamp. Fine porcelain gives up a fifth of a millimetre to reach a clean face; coarse stoneware wants better than half. The loss is entered on the piece's line in the register.

    Damp paper goes on next and is tamped into the relief with a stiff short brush, struck rather than stroked, until the sheet has taken the shape of everything under it. Then the ink pounce — silk over cotton wadding — worked over the standing ground in small circles from the centre outward, so the pattern comes up white on black and the reader can see where the paper failed to seat.

    The sheet is lifted while it is still damp, dried on the line, numbered with the piece's admission number and the face number, and bound into an album. Readers work from albums. The piece goes back into its coffer with the face it now has.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :limits, heading: "What A Face Costs"
    Water is issued in 合, a tenth of a 升 and so a tenth of a litre. A full face costs three: two for the paper and one for the plate. What the plate loses to the air is not issued at all, which is why squeezes are taken at night and why a reader who spoils a sheet waits for the next issue rather than the next hour.

    Paper is the tighter constraint. Sheets come in with the couriers, the settlements do not make them, and the bench at the #{ref :juyan_register_house, "Juyan Register House"} runs out of paper several times a year and has never once run out of water in the middle of a face.

    Each reading spends a face. A piece holds a countable number of them, the number is on its line, and a reading cannot be repeated once the face that gave it has been lapped away. Readers hold to one class of ware — porcelain, coarse stoneware, or the mineral-loaded slabs that take the finest pattern and shatter under a badly struck brush — and a reader does not take a piece outside their class. A brush struck wrong on a slab takes the face under it and the two beneath that.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — Reading is a stone-rubbing: damp paper tamped into relief and inked with a pounce, from the epigraphers' practice of taking 拓本 squeezes off carved stelae. Nobody in the settlements does epigraphy; the mechanism was borrowed because it recovers a pattern from a hard surface with no power and no instrument, and it made the readable object a sheet of paper rather than the piece."
  log "2026-08-09 — The water allowance is stated in 合, a tenth of a 升, taken from pre-modern Chinese volume measure rather than millilitres: the settlements issue against stamped cards written in units their statutes already use, and three 合 a face gives the reading queue a rate the well line can be compared against."
end
